import { urls } from "../../../../data";
<div id="signal-overview">
  <component-banner-img/>

  <p>
    A Signal is a small, non-interactive label that tells the user one fact
    about the item or object it's attached to, such as "Great price", "Shipped"
    or "Deal ending".
  </p>

  <h2 id="signal-usage">When to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      To state one short fact that helps the user understand an item or decide:
      a status, a favorable value, a time limit or background context.
    </li>
    <li>
      On item tiles, and in cards, list rows, page headers and other places
      where an object needs a compact label.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="signal-usage-not">When not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      For anything interactive or clickable. A Signal has no focus, hover or
      pressed state.
    </li>
    <li>
      For counts or presence indicators on another element. Use
      <a href=`${urls.components}/badge`>badge</a> instead.
    </li>
    <li>For free-form or marketing copy. Use approved strings.</li>
    <li>On many items at once. Overuse removes the meaning of a Signal.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="signal-tones">Tones</h2>
  <p>
    A Signal has five tones. Each tone has one meaning everywhere it appears.
  </p>
  <div
    class="table table--density-compact"
    role="group"
    aria-labelledby="signal-tones"
    tabindex="0"
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="table-cell">Tone</th>
          <th class="table-cell">Color</th>
          <th class="table-cell">Meaning</th>
          <th class="table-cell">Examples</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><strong>Neutral</strong></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Gray</td>
          <td class="table-cell">
            This is context. A standard state or background, without judgment.
          </td>
          <td class="table-cell">"Draft", "Ended", "Small business"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><strong>Information</strong></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Blue</td>
          <td class="table-cell">
            This is worth knowing. Something new, an option, activity or
            assurance that could help the user decide.
          </td>
          <td class="table-cell">
            "New listing", "Trending", "Authenticity guarantee"
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><strong>Positive</strong></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Green</td>
          <td class="table-cell">
            This is favorable. The message says something is good: a benefit, a
            good value or a successful outcome.
          </td>
          <td class="table-cell">"Great price", "Free shipping", "Shipped"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><strong>Warning</strong></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Yellow</td>
          <td class="table-cell">
            Be aware of this. An unresolved condition or requirement with a
            possible consequence.
          </td>
          <td class="table-cell">
            "Pending", "Under review", "Ship by [date]"
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><strong>Attention</strong></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Red</td>
          <td class="table-cell">
            Notice this now. An immediate consequence: limited time or
            availability, a failure, or something blocked.
          </td>
          <td class="table-cell">
            "Last one", "Deal ending", "Shipment overdue"
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Choose the tone from what the Signal means in its context, not from the text
    alone. For example, "Sold" is positive for a seller but not for a buyer.
  </p>
  <p>
    To pick a tone, ask in this order: is there an immediate consequence
    (attention), a possible one (warning), does the message say something is
    favorable (positive), or could it change what the user does next
    (information). Otherwise use neutral.
  </p>

  <h2 id="signal-emphasis">Emphasis</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Default:</strong> start here. Use it for most Signals, including
      Signals that repeat across many items.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Strong:</strong> use only when the Signal is rare on the page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Subtle:</strong> text only, with no fill, border or padding.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Emphasis changes visual weight, never meaning. Don't change the tone to get
    more emphasis. To make "Great price" stand out, keep it positive and use
    strong.
  </p>

  <h2 id="signal-content">Content</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use approved Signal strings. New wording needs content review.</li>
    <li>Keep labels to one to three words.</li>
    <li>Use sentence case. Don't use all caps.</li>
    <li>
      Long labels wrap onto more lines when space is narrow. Where a single line
      is required, the label can truncate with an ellipsis instead.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="signal-v1">Signal V1 (deprecated)</h2>
  <p>
    These Signal V1 classes are deprecated aliases. They render the V2 look with
    the mapped tone at default emphasis, and are removed in the next major
    release of Skin.
  </p>
  <div
    class="table table--density-compact"
    role="group"
    aria-labelledby="signal-v1"
    tabindex="0"
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="table-cell">V1 class</th>
          <th class="table-cell">V2 tone</th>
          <th class="table-cell">V2 emphasis</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><code>signal--trustworthy</code></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Information</td>
          <td class="table-cell">Default</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><code>signal--recent</code></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Positive</td>
          <td class="table-cell">Default</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><code>signal--time-sensitive</code></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Attention</td>
          <td class="table-cell">Default</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="table-cell"><code>signal--neutral</code></td>
          <td class="table-cell">Neutral</td>
          <td class="table-cell">Default</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>The mapping keeps today's colors, not semantic correctness.</strong>
    Domain teams must review their Signals and correct the tone and emphasis.
    For example, "New listing" was <code>recent</code> (positive) but should be
    information.
  </p>
  <p>
    Move migrated labels from uppercase to sentence case. See the
    <a href=`${urls.components}/signal/css#signal-v1-deprecated`>CSS tab</a> for
    the class replacements.
  </p>
</div>
